WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.238
This page describes one more property of
receptors.

00:00:03.885 --> 00:00:06.713
You will need it every time you read a
dose-response curve.

00:00:07.505 --> 00:00:10.026
Occupancy and response are two different
measurements.

00:00:10.605 --> 00:00:14.025
Occupancy is the fraction of receptors that have
ligand bound.

00:00:14.595 --> 00:00:16.200
Response is what the tissue does.

00:00:16.756 --> 00:00:23.255
In 1956, Nickerson published a short paper in
Nature called "Receptor occupancy and tissue
response."

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:28.954
He reported that a tissue can reach its full
response before all of its receptors are
occupied.

00:00:29.535 --> 00:00:32.735
The reason is amplification in the pathway after
the receptor.

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:41.484
Once enough receptors are occupied to saturate
the slowest step of the pathway, more occupied
receptors change nothing that you can measure.

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:45.162
The receptors that are not needed are called
spare receptors.

00:00:45.758 --> 00:00:47.567
This has a practical consequence.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.439
The EC50 is the concentration that gives half of
the maximum response.

00:00:53.038 --> 00:00:54.851
It is a property of the whole pathway.

00:00:55.375 --> 00:00:59.285
The Kd is the concentration that occupies half
of the receptors.

00:00:59.894 --> 00:01:01.504
It is a property of the receptor.

00:01:02.254 --> 00:01:07.875
A common error in the research literature is to
treat an EC50 as if it were a binding affinity.

00:01:08.572 --> 00:01:10.489
You can also use the difference as a tool.

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:16.032
Suppose a treatment changes a drug's EC50 but
does not change its binding affinity.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.471
Then something after binding has changed, and
you can look for it.
